PLACES=[
N('nancy-brown-peace-carillon-belle-isle','Nancy Brown Peace Carillon','Detroit','mi','landmark','standing',42.3352,-82.98851,'exact','public','walk','','1939–1940',
  'An eight-sided Gothic Revival bell tower, 98 feet tall, on Belle Isle near the band shell. It is named for Nancy Brown, the pen name of a Detroit News columnist who held huge sunrise services on the island from 1934 and raised the whole cost, about $60,000, from her readers. Ground was broken in October 1939 and it was dedicated on June 16, 1940. Bells cost too much, so it was built to sound them electrically; new chimes went in in 2003.',
  'The eight-sided Gothic tower. Wikipedia says some of the stained glass was broken by vandals by 1970, and a Detroit writer said in 2013 that it had long been neglected; its condition now was not checked. You see it from the outside; there is no word of the inside being open.',
  'A newspaper columnist\'s readers paid for every stone of it.',
  BI_ACC+' The tower stands beside Loiter Way; the nearest mapped parking lot is about 100 m west of it.',
  [W('Nancy Brown Peace Carillon')]+BI_LINKS,
  th=[42.33532,-82.98980],thn='Parking lot on Loiter Way',
  brick='?',
  arch='Harley, Ellington and Day',archw='Wikipedia: designed by the architectural firm of Harley, Ellington, and Day'),
N('belle-isle-conservatory-detroit','Anna Scripps Whitcomb Conservatory (Belle Isle Conservatory)','Detroit','mi','inst','standing',42.33625,-82.98554,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1904',
  'A domed glass greenhouse and botanical garden in the middle of Belle Isle, built from 1902 to 1904 and opened in 1904; Wikipedia calls it the oldest conservatory in the United States that has run without a break. It was modelled on the great glass houses of the 1800s, the Crystal Palace and the Palm House at Kew. It is named for Anna Scripps Whitcomb, who left her 600 orchids to the city in 1955. It reopened in 2024 after a renovation.',
  'The central palm house under the dome, with wings for tropical plants, cacti and ferns and a show house, a lily and koi pond to the north and perennial beds to the west, with the Levi L. Barbour fountain by Marshall Fredericks. The DNR lists no fee for it.',
  'The glass house on the island, older than the cars that made the city.',
  BI_ACC+' Hours and closed days from the DNR\'s park page. Parking along Inselruhe Avenue and Conservatory Drive.',
  [W('Belle Isle Conservatory')]+BI_LINKS,
  th=[42.33727,-82.98569],thn='Parking lot on Inselruhe Avenue',
  brick='?',
  arch='George D. Mason; Albert Kahn',archw='Wikipedia: designed by the firm of George D. Mason and Albert Kahn, with the neighbouring aquarium',
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Dec. 21–22, Christmas Eve and Day, Dec. 28–29, New Year\'s Eve and Day'),
N('book-tower-detroit','Book Tower','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33349,-83.05153,'exact','open','walk','','1925–1926',
  'A 38-storey Italian Renaissance office tower on Washington Boulevard, built in 1925 and 1926 as an addition to the Book Building for the Book brothers, who owned much of the street. It was the tallest building in Detroit until the Penobscot Building rose in 1928. An 81-storey tower planned for the other end of the Book Building was given up in the Depression. It declined from the 1970s and stood empty; after a restoration finished in the 2020s it holds apartments and a hotel and reopened to the public in 2023.',
  'Plainer lower floors with the ornament gathering toward the top. The hotel inside is ROOST Detroit; interior not checked.',
  'Detroit\'s tallest building for two years, empty for years after, and open again.',
  'Privately owned; 1265 Washington Boulevard, in the Washington Boulevard Historic District. A hotel (ROOST Detroit) works inside; its hours were not read. Street and garage parking downtown.',
  [W('Book Tower')],
  brick='?',
  arch='Louis Kamper',archw='Wikipedia: designed by Louis Kamper in the Italian Renaissance style',
  hours='?'),
N('book-cadillac-hotel-detroit','Westin Book Cadillac Hotel','Detroit','mi','hotel','standing',42.33223,-83.05021,'exact','open','walk','','1924–',
  'A 31-storey hotel on Washington Boulevard, opened as the Book-Cadillac on December 8, 1924, when it was the tallest building in the city and the tallest hotel in the world. The Book brothers built it on the site of the Cadillac Hotel of 1888, which closed in June 1923 and was torn down. Sheraton ran it from 1951. It closed in October 1984 for a renovation that never came, its contents were sold off in 1986, and it reopened in October 2008 as a Westin after a $200 million restoration, with condominiums on the top eight floors.',
  'The Neo-Renaissance tower and, inside, the lobby and ballrooms of the first floors. It is a working hotel; how much of the original interior survives was not checked.',
  'The world\'s tallest hotel in 1924, a ruin by the 2000s, a hotel again.',
  'A working hotel at 1114 Washington Boulevard at Michigan Avenue, run by Westin. Hotel parking and downtown garages.',
  [W('Westin Book Cadillac Hotel')],
  brick='?',
  arch='Louis Kamper',archw='Wikipedia: the Books commissioned architect Louis Kamper to design their hotel',
  hours='?'),
N('broderick-tower-detroit','Broderick Tower (Eaton Tower)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33595,-83.04973,'exact','view','walk','','1926–1928',
  'A 34-storey tower at Woodward Avenue and Witherell Street, facing Grand Circus Park, built from 1926 to 1928 as the Eaton Tower for Theodore H. Eaton Jr., a dealer in chemicals and dyes; it was then the second tallest building in Michigan. The insurance broker David Broderick bought it in 1945 and gave it his name. It was fully renovated as apartments in 2012, and a sale and another renovation were reported in 2025.',
  'A limestone-faced tower with a Beaux-Arts crown; the decorative cornices were removed at some time. With the David Whitney Building across Woodward it makes a gateway to downtown seen from the north. Apartments inside: seen from the street.',
  '',
  'Privately owned apartments, 10 Witherell Street at Woodward Avenue. See it from Grand Circus Park, a city park across the street.',
  [W('David Broderick Tower')],
  brick='?',
  arch='Louis Kamper; Paul Kamper',archw='Wikipedia: designed by architects Louis Kamper and Paul Kamper for Theodore Eaton'),
N('david-whitney-building-detroit','David Whitney Building','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33558,-83.05042,'exact','open','walk','','1914–',
  'A 19-storey office building on a wedge-shaped lot where Park Avenue, Woodward Avenue and Washington Boulevard meet, begun in 1914 and named for David Whitney Jr., a lumber baron. It was designed by Graham, Burnham and Company, the firm that followed D. H. Burnham\'s; the claim that Daniel Burnham himself designed it began with a 1950s newspaper article, and he died in 1912. Its cornices were replaced with a plain top in 1959. A $92 million renovation, finished in December 2014, made it a hotel with apartments above and restored the four-storey atrium.',
  'Terra cotta and glazed brick outside; inside, the four-storey atrium on the first floors, now the hotel\'s lobby. Further hotel works were announced in 2022; what the atrium looks like now was not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned; an Aloft hotel with apartments above, 1 Park Avenue on Grand Circus Park. Hotel hours not read. Downtown parking garages.',
  [W('David Whitney Building')],
  brick='no',bcol='glazed',
  arch='Graham, Burnham & Co.',archw='Wikipedia: the structure was designed by Graham, Burnham & Co., the successor firm to the D.H. Burnham Company',
  hours='?'),
N('dime-building-chrysler-house-detroit','Dime Building (Chrysler House)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33079,-83.04802,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1912',
  'A 23-storey office building at 719 Griswold Street in the Financial District, built from 1910 to 1912 as the Dime Savings Bank Building. It has had several names (the Commonwealth Building, Griswold Place, the Dime Building again from 2002) and since 2012 has been Chrysler House. The Lincoln Highway Association had its first national headquarters in office 2115 from 1913 to 1928.',
  'A steel frame faced with white glazed brick and terra cotta trim. A later renovation refaced the two lowest floors in grey granite and took off the pediment over the entrance and the cornice. Offices, with shops at street level; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned offices, 719 Griswold Street next to the Penobscot Building. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Chrysler House')],
  brick='no',bcol='glazed',
  arch='Daniel Burnham',archw='Wikipedia\'s article on Chrysler House names Daniel Burnham as its architect'),
N('old-main-wayne-state-detroit','Old Main (Central High School)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.35493,-83.06751,'exact','view','walk','','1894–1896',
  'Built as Detroit\'s Central High School at Cass and Warren avenues, begun on December 13, 1894, of brick and limestone, with 103 classrooms for 2,000 students. The school had been in the old state capitol in Capitol Park, which burned in 1893. Classes began here in 1896. The building later housed the college that grew into Wayne State University, and it is now the university\'s College of Liberal Arts and Sciences.',
  'The towered school building, renovated with much of it restored to its first look; the tower clock was replaced in 1934. It is a working university building; whether visitors may walk in was not checked.',
  'The high school that became a university.',
  'Wayne State University, 4841 Cass Avenue at Warren Avenue, Midtown. University parking structures nearby; seen from the street.',
  [W('Old Main (Wayne State University)')],
  brick='?',
  arch='Malcomson and Higginbotham',archw='Wikipedia: designed by architects Malcomson & Higginbotham'),
N('wayne-county-building-detroit','Wayne County Building (old Wayne County Courthouse)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33213,-83.04272,'exact','view','walk','','1897–1902',
  'The old county building and courthouse at 600 Randolph Street, built from 1897 to 1902 and called on completion one of the most sumptuous buildings in Michigan. It is Roman Baroque Revival with Beaux-Arts touches, with a tall central tower and end pavilions; the copper dome and spire were redone in the 1960s. The county\'s offices moved to the Guardian Building after 2008, the county commission approved its sale in 2014, and a $7 million restoration of its masonry, windows and lighting ran from 2016 to 2018. It was put up for sale again in 2021.',
  'Buff Berea sandstone over a rusticated base, a broad stair to a two-storey Corinthian portico, the Anthony Wayne pediment, and copper groups of Victory and Progress driving chariots. Wikipedia says the inside is finished in woods, marbles, tiles and mosaics; whether it opens to the public was not checked.',
  'The chariots on the roof.',
  'Privately owned, 600 Randolph Street, facing Cadillac Square. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Wayne County Building')],
  brick='no',
  arch='John Scott; Arthur Scott',archw='Wikipedia: designed by Detroit architects John and Arthur Scott'),
N('cadillac-place-gm-building-detroit','Cadillac Place (General Motors Building)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.36869,-83.07534,'exact','view','walk','','1919–',
  'General Motors\' headquarters in New Center, on West Grand Boulevard between Cass and Second, begun in June 1919; the Cass Avenue wing was in use by November 1920. It was to be named for William C. Durant, who was pushed out in 1921, so the D already carved over the entrance stayed. GM moved out, and a renovation of 2000 to 2002 made it state offices, renamed Cadillac Place after the founder of Detroit. It is a National Historic Landmark (1985).',
  'A huge many-winged block faced in limestone and crowned with a two-storey Corinthian colonnade; inside, an arcade and an elevator lobby with a coffered ceiling. State offices: seen from the street, interior not checked.',
  'Look for the D over the door.',
  'State of Michigan offices, 3044 West Grand Boulevard, New Center. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Cadillac Place')],
  brick='no',
  arch='Albert Kahn',archw='Wikipedia: Albert Kahn was hired as architect'),
N('gar-building-detroit','Grand Army of the Republic Building','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33502,-83.05498,'exact','view','walk','','1897–',
  'A five-storey Richardsonian Romanesque building like a small castle, on a triangular lot at Grand River Avenue and Cass, begun in 1897 for the Civil War veterans of the Grand Army of the Republic, who paid $6,000 of its $44,000 cost; the city paid the rest. It held shops and a bank, offices and a small hall. The veterans gave it to the city in 1934. After years of court cases over its deed it was sold in 2011 to a media company that restored it and opened restaurants; those closed after the pandemic, and it was sold again in February 2023.',
  'The stone turrets and battlements on its triangular corner. Its use now and the inside were not checked.',
  'A castle downtown built for Civil War veterans.',
  'Privately owned, 1942 Grand River Avenue at Cass Avenue. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Grand Army of the Republic Building (Detroit)')],
  brick='?',
  arch='Julius Hess',archw='Wikipedia: designed by architect Julius Hess'),
N('guardian-building-detroit','Guardian Building (Union Trust Building)','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.32956,-83.04608,'exact','view','walk','','1928–1929',
  'A 43-storey Art Deco office tower in the Financial District, built in 1928 and 1929 as the Union Trust Building and nicknamed the Cathedral of Finance for its tower over the entrance and its rounded far end. It is a National Historic Landmark (1989), and Wayne County owns it and has its offices there.',
  'Outside, coloured brick (sold afterwards as "Union Trust Brick" and later "Guardian brick"), tile, limestone and terra cotta in patterns, with sculptures by Corrado Parducci at the Griswold Street door. Inside, a three-storey vaulted lobby in Pewabic and Rookwood tile and a Monel metal screen with a Tiffany clock. Wikipedia says an owner in the 2000s reopened the lobby to the public; whether and when it is open now was not checked.',
  'The cathedral of finance and its tiled lobby.',
  'Wayne County\'s headquarters, 500 Griswold Street. Seen from the street; lobby hours not checked.',
  [W('Guardian Building'),['The Guardian Building','https://www.guardianbuilding.com/']],
  brick='?',
  arch='Smith, Hinchman and Grylls',archw='Wikipedia: Wirt C. Rowland, of the Smith, Hinchman & Grylls firm, was the building\'s architect'),
N('leland-hotel-detroit','Detroit-Leland Hotel (The Leland)','Detroit','mi','hotel','standing',42.33367,-83.05442,'exact','view','walk','','1926–1927',
  'A 22-storey hotel at 400 Bagley Street, built from 1926 and opened in April 1927 with 800 rooms; it is named for Henry M. Leland, who founded Cadillac. Its owners went bankrupt by 1929, but it kept open through it. Its ballroom has held a nightclub since 1983, and it no longer takes overnight guests. In June 2026 a bankruptcy judge approved its sale for $3 million; tenants had been put out by a power failure in 2025.',
  'Brick, granite and terra cotta on the lower floors and buff brick from the fourth floor up, with a flat roof. Seen from the street; the inside was not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned, 400 Bagley Street at Cass Avenue. Seen from the street.',
  [W('The Leland Hotel (Detroit)')],
  brick='no',bcol='buff',
  arch='Rapp and Rapp',builder='Lundoff-Bicknell',archw='Wikipedia: Rapp & Rapp designed the building, and the Cleveland firm of Lundoff-Bicknell were the contractors'),
N('detroit-masonic-temple','Detroit Masonic Temple','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.34174,-83.06018,'exact','open','walk','','1920–1926',
  'Called the largest Masonic temple in the world: a Gothic Revival block of Indiana limestone at 500 Temple Street in the Cass Corridor, with 16 floors and 1,037 rooms, three theatres, ballrooms, a drill hall and a swimming pool. Ground was broken in November 1920, the cornerstone laid on September 19, 1922 with the trowel George Washington used at the United States Capitol, and it opened in 1926. On the National Register since 1980.',
  'The limestone tower facing Cass Park. Inside are the theatre, the halls and lodge rooms, seen on a guided tour.',
  'A temple of a thousand rooms.',
  'Owned by the Masonic Temple Association, 500 Temple Street. Guided tours are sold online in advance; the temple\'s tour page says the dates vary with staff and are announced two to four weeks ahead. Concerts and shows in the theatre are separate.',
  [W('Detroit Masonic Temple'),['Detroit Masonic Temple: building tours','https://www.themasonic.com/-building-tours']],
  brick='no',
  arch='George D. Mason',archw='Wikipedia: Architect George D. Mason designed the whole structure',
  hours='Guided tours on set dates, announced two to four weeks ahead; tickets online in advance'),
N('penobscot-building-detroit','Penobscot Building','Detroit','mi','civic','standing',42.33027,-83.04755,'exact','view','walk','','1927–1928',
  'A 45-storey Art Deco office tower in the Financial District, built in 1927 and 1928 and the tallest building in Michigan until the Renaissance Center of 1977; when finished it was the eighth tallest in the world. It is named for the Penobscot River in Maine, where the lumberman Simon J. Murphy, whose company built it, had worked. It shares the block with the first Penobscot Building and an annex.',
  'Indiana limestone over a granite base, rising sheer for thirty storeys and then stepping back to a red neon beacon. American Indian motifs in the entrance arch and the lobby metalwork, and sculpture by Corrado Parducci. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned offices, 645 Griswold Street. Seen from the street.',
  [W('Penobscot Building')],
  brick='no',
  arch='Smith, Hinchman and Grylls',archw='Wikipedia: the architect Wirt C. Rowland, of the Smith Hinchman & Grylls firm, designed the Penobscot'),
N('old-st-marys-church-detroit','Old St. Mary\'s Church (St. Mary Roman Catholic Church)','Detroit','mi','worship','standing',42.33559,-83.04036,'exact','view','walk','','1884–1885',
  'The church of the third oldest Catholic parish in Detroit, founded in 1834 for German-speaking immigrants, at 646 Monroe Street in Greektown. The first church on the site was built in 1841 on land the Beaubien family sold for a dollar; the present one, designed by Peter Dederichs, was begun in 1884 and finished in 1885. The school (1868) and rectory (1876) stand with it.',
  'Red brick in the Romanesque style with Venetian touches; twin towers frame a rose window on the west front. Inside, ten polished granite columns, each from one piece of stone, divide the aisles. Mass times were not read from the parish\'s own page, which could not be opened when this was written.',
  'A German parish church in the middle of Greektown.',
  'An active Catholic parish, 646 Monroe Street at St. Antoine Street. Seen from the street; go in at Mass times, which were not checked.',
  [W('St. Mary Roman Catholic Church (Detroit)')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',
  arch='Peter Dederichs',archw='Wikipedia: designed by German-born Peter Dederichs'),
N('alger-house-grosse-pointe-war-memorial','Russell A. Alger Jr. House (Grosse Pointe War Memorial)','Grosse Pointe Farms','mi','landmark','standing',42.38667,-82.89751,'exact','open','drive','','1910–',
  'A lakeside house called the Moorings, designed in 1910 for Russell A. Alger Jr., one of the founders of the Packard Motor Car Company, and his wife Marion; he lived here until his death in 1930. It was given to the Detroit Institute of Arts in 1936, returned to the family in 1948 and given in 1949 to the Grosse Pointe War Memorial Association as a memorial to those who served in the Second World War. An auditorium and ballroom were added in 1962 and an arts centre in 1993.',
  'A symmetrical stucco house with a hipped roof, two storeys to the street and three to the lake, with a rusticated stone entrance. Inside, a marble entry, a stone staircase, and a reception hall with a herringbone wood floor and a stone fireplace. Wikipedia says the house and its landscaping are in original condition.',
  '',
  'The Grosse Pointe War Memorial, a community centre at 32 Lake Shore Road, Grosse Pointe Farms. It holds classes, concerts and events; its hours were not found on its front page.',
  [W('Grosse Pointe War Memorial'),['The War Memorial','https://www.warmemorial.org/']],
  brick='no',
  arch='Charles A. Platt',archw='Wikipedia: the couple commissioned Charles A. Platt to design this house',
  hours='?'),
]

